    "bio": "Eiko Kawashima (川島 栄子, Kawashima Eiko; born 31 August 1961), better known as Anri (杏里), is a Japanese pop singer from Yamato, Kanagawa Prefecture. In a career spanning more than 40 years she achieved commercial success and popularity in the city pop music scene during the 1980s. Her debut release was 1978's \"Oribia o Kikinagara\" (\"While Listening to Olivia\"), written by Amii Ozaki. She broke through with her signature 1983 album Timely!!, which spawned the hits \"Cat's Eye\" and \"Kanashimi ga Tomaranai\". Throughout the 1980s and early 1990s her albums were bestsellers, including a tour with attendance of almost 100,000. With the renewed popularity of city pop — and her songs having been sampled by many new vaporwave and future funk artists — Anri has enjoyed a second wave of success outside Japan, and several of her albums were reissued in 2023.",
    "highlights": [
      { "year": "1983", "text": "\"Cat's Eye\" debuted at No. 1 on Countdown Japan and spent 4 weeks at No. 1 on the Oricon chart. It was the first opening theme of the eponymous anime series, one of the earliest J-pop anime tie-ins." },
      { "year": "1983", "text": "\"Timely!!\" became her first No. 1 album, propelled by \"Cat's Eye\" and \"Kanashimi ga Tomaranai\" (\"I Can't Stop the Loneliness\")." },
      { "year": "1987–88", "text": "\"Summer Candles\" and \"Dolphin Ring\" became standard songs at Japanese weddings and receptions." },
      { "year": "1987", "text": "Toured Hawaii for the first time, and played a tour that drew close to 100,000 attendees." },
      { "year": "1998", "text": "Sang \"Furusato\" during the closing ceremony of the 1998 Winter Olympics in Nagano." },
      { "year": "2002", "text": "Began collaborating with jazz fusion guitarist Lee Ritenour, who produced the LP Smooth Jam – Quiet Storm." },
      { "year": "2021", "text": "Teamed up with Mariya Takeuchi on \"Watching Over You\" as \"Peach & Apricot\"." },
      { "year": "2020s", "text": "City pop revival: her catalogue is sampled by vaporwave and future funk artists, and several albums were reissued in 2023." }
    ]
  },
